{"type": "message", "id": "34b27983", "parentId": "f200b14d", "timestamp": "2026-04-04T03:53:32.998Z", "message": {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_a5756c60323b4cd3ac7f6952", "toolName": "web_search", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\n  \"query\": \"esophageal cancer 5-year survival rate 2024 statistics\",\n  \"provider\": \"brave\",\n  \"count\": 5,\n  \"tookMs\": 894,\n  \"externalContent\": {\n    \"untrusted\": true,\n    \"source\": \"web_search\",\n    \"provider\": \"brave\",\n    \"wrapped\": true\n  },\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"28cd0824f54f35ab\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nCancer Stat Facts: Esophageal Cancer - SEER\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"28cd0824f54f35ab\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://seer.cancer.gov/statfacts/html/esoph.html\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"1d4502363ba30646\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThe earlier esophageal cancer is ... esophageal cancer, 18.6% are diagnosed at the local stage. <strong>The 5-year relative survival for localized esophageal cancer is 48.7%</strong>....\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"1d4502363ba30646\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"seer.cancer.gov\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"1128473b3f37c3d4\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nSurvival Rates for Esophageal Cancer | American Cancer Society\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"1128473b3f37c3d4\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.cancer.org/cancer/types/esophagus-cancer/detection-diagnosis-staging/survival-rates.html\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"8911c96042b3f5b9\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nFor example, if the 5-year relative survival rate for a specific stage of esophageal cancer is 60%, it means that people who have that cancer are, on average, <strong>about 60%</strong> as likely as people who don’t have that cancer to live for at least 5 years after being diagnosed.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"8911c96042b3f5b9\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"January 13, 2026\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.cancer.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"5e25db184709096d\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nRecommendations for broadening eligibility criteria in esophagus cancer clinical trials: the mortality disparity of esophagus cancer as a first or second primary malignancy - PMC\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"5e25db184709096d\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11228734/\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"237d1aba7cffdf69\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nEsophageal cancer (EC) ranks as the seventh most prevalent cancer globally and stands as the sixth leading cause of cancer-related mortality worldwide, with around 544,000 reported deaths in 2020 (1). For all stages of EC combined, the 5-year survival rate hovers <strong>around 20%</strong>. In the United States, ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"237d1aba7cffdf69\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"76c081c7df0000c6\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nEsophageal Cancer: An Updated Surveillance Epidemiology and End Results Database Analysis - PMC\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"76c081c7df0000c6\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7141161/\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"c0b623f6dd2f49a7\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThis disease is the sixth cause of cancer-related deaths and the eighth most common cancer worldwide with a 5-year survival rate of <strong>less than 25%</strong> [1]. An estimated 17,650 cases of esophageal cancer will be diagnosed each year in the USA, from which 16,080 deaths are expected [2]. Squamous cell ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"c0b623f6dd2f49a7\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"9b98027deb7821d7\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nTrends in incidence, treatment modalities and prognosis of esophageal adenocarcinoma in the US population - ScienceDirect\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"9b98027deb7821d7\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1877782124001620\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"757ac6bfee4197c5\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThe 5-year overall survival of EAC was still <strong>less than 25 %</strong>, but 1-year relative survival has moderately increased to 53.7 %. ... A shift in the treatment modalities was observed in EAC patients with and without distant metastasis.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"757ac6bfee4197c5\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"October 3, 2024\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.sciencedirect.com\"\n    }\n  ]\n}"}], "details": {"query": "esophageal cancer 5-year survival rate 2024 statistics", "provider": "brave", "count": 5, "tookMs": 894, "externalContent": {"untrusted": true, "source": "web_search", "provider": "brave", "wrapped": true}, "results": [{"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"28cd0824f54f35ab\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nCancer Stat Facts: Esophageal Cancer - SEER\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"28cd0824f54f35ab\">>>", "url": "https://seer.cancer.gov/statfacts/html/esoph.html", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"1d4502363ba30646\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nThe earlier esophageal cancer is ... esophageal cancer, 18.6% are diagnosed at the local stage. <strong>The 5-year relative survival for localized esophageal cancer is 48.7%</strong>....\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"1d4502363ba30646\">>>", "siteName": "seer.cancer.gov"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"1128473b3f37c3d4\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nSurvival Rates for Esophageal Cancer | American Cancer Society\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"1128473b3f37c3d4\">>>", "url": "https://www.cancer.org/cancer/types/esophagus-cancer/detection-diagnosis-staging/survival-rates.html", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"8911c96042b3f5b9\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nFor example, if the 5-year relative survival rate for a specific stage of esophageal cancer is 60%, it means that people who have that cancer are, on average, <strong>about 60%</strong> as likely as people who don’t have that cancer to live for at least 5 years after being diagnosed.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"8911c96042b3f5b9\">>>", "published": "January 13, 2026", "siteName": "www.cancer.org"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"5e25db184709096d\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nRecommendations for broadening eligibility criteria in esophagus cancer clinical trials: the mortality disparity of esophagus cancer as a first or second primary malignancy - PMC\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"5e25db184709096d\">>>", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11228734/", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"237d1aba7cffdf69\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nEsophageal cancer (EC) ranks as the seventh most prevalent cancer globally and stands as the sixth leading cause of cancer-related mortality worldwide, with around 544,000 reported deaths in 2020 (1). For all stages of EC combined, the 5-year survival rate hovers <strong>around 20%</strong>. In the United States, ...\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"237d1aba7cffdf69\">>>", "siteName": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"76c081c7df0000c6\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nEsophageal Cancer: An Updated Surveillance Epidemiology and End Results Database Analysis - PMC\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"76c081c7df0000c6\">>>", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7141161/", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"c0b623f6dd2f49a7\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nThis disease is the sixth cause of cancer-related deaths and the eighth most common cancer worldwide with a 5-year survival rate of <strong>less than 25%</strong> [1]. An estimated 17,650 cases of esophageal cancer will be diagnosed each year in the USA, from which 16,080 deaths are expected [2]. Squamous cell ...\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"c0b623f6dd2f49a7\">>>", "siteName": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"9b98027deb7821d7\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nTrends in incidence, treatment modalities and prognosis of esophageal adenocarcinoma in the US population - ScienceDirect\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"9b98027deb7821d7\">>>", "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1877782124001620", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"757ac6bfee4197c5\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nThe 5-year overall survival of EAC was still <strong>less than 25 %</strong>, but 1-year relative survival has moderately increased to 53.7 %. ... A shift in the treatment modalities was observed in EAC patients with and without distant metastasis.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"757ac6bfee4197c5\">>>", "published": "October 3, 2024", "siteName": "www.sciencedirect.com"}]}, "isError": false, "timestamp": 1775274812997}}
{"type": "message", "id": "83491e49", "parentId": "34b27983", "timestamp": "2026-04-04T03:53:33.171Z", "message": {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_66b2a8e1029a441fa7e1e25b", "toolName": "web_search", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\n  \"query\": \"brain cancer glioblastoma 5-year survival rate statistics\",\n  \"provider\": \"brave\",\n  \"count\": 5,\n  \"tookMs\": 1067,\n  \"externalContent\": {\n    \"untrusted\": true,\n    \"source\": \"web_search\",\n    \"provider\": \"brave\",\n    \"wrapped\": true\n  },\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"55c8352b619d4b49\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nGlioblastoma Prognosis | Survival Rates\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"55c8352b619d4b49\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.thebraintumourcharity.org/brain-tumour-diagnosis-treatment/types-of-brain-tumour-adult/glioblastoma/glioblastoma-prognosis/\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"f8ea38bde8a2e671\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nHere are the current statistics we have on glioblastoma prognosis: ... Average survival rate: 25% of patients survive more than one year, and <strong>more than 5%</strong> of patients survive five years or more\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"f8ea38bde8a2e671\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"January 22, 2019\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.thebraintumourcharity.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"76f9eef951e9040b\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nGlioblastoma and Increased Survival with Longer Chemotherapy Duration - PMC\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"76f9eef951e9040b\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6710024/\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"ea9ebf61ec33f9e9\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThe most prevalent locations of all brain tumors were the frontal lobe (25.6%) and the temporal lobe (19.6%). The CBTRUS also reported the mortality rate from malignant brain and CNS tumors was <strong>3.48 for females and 5.23 for males per 100,000 person-years. The five-year survival rate for GBM is 4.7%</strong>...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"ea9ebf61ec33f9e9\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"7254222dbabe39dd\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nWhat is the average glioblastoma survival rate? — Glioblastoma Research Organization\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"7254222dbabe39dd\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.gbmresearch.org/blog/glioblastoma-survival-rate\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"690a28c2ea7a0cef\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nChildren diagnosed with glioblastoma are predicted to have a better survival rate, and <strong>about 25 percent</strong> of kids who have this tumor live for five years or more. A lack of data raises many questions about the most up-to-date information.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"690a28c2ea7a0cef\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"December 1, 2022\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.gbmresearch.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"17c7f9f7baa848da\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nGlioblastoma - Survival rates - Mayo Clinic\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"17c7f9f7baa848da\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/glioblastoma/survival-rates/gnc-20596050\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"cc3bce602b6d481b\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nGlioblastoma — Overview covers diagnosis and treatment of this aggressive form of brain cancer that can be slowed but usually can&#x27;t be cured.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"cc3bce602b6d481b\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"1 month ago\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.mayoclinic.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"119b02725f301828\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nGlioblastoma (GBM): What It Is, Symptoms & Prognosis\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"119b02725f301828\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/17032-glioblastoma\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"bdbc8a3aa058c2b3\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nMost people live an average of 12 to 18 months after diagnosis. The five-year survival rate for glioblastoma is only <strong>about 5%</strong>. That means <strong>about 5%</strong> of people with GBM are still alive five years after their diagnosis.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"bdbc8a3aa058c2b3\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"August 30, 2023\",\n      \"siteName\": \"my.clevelandclinic.org\"\n    }\n  ]\n}"}], "details": {"query": "brain cancer glioblastoma 5-year survival rate statistics", "provider": "brave", "count": 5, "tookMs": 1067, "externalContent": {"untrusted": true, "source": "web_search", "provider": "brave", "wrapped": true}, "results": [{"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"55c8352b619d4b49\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nGlioblastoma Prognosis | Survival Rates\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"55c8352b619d4b49\">>>", "url": "https://www.thebraintumourcharity.org/brain-tumour-diagnosis-treatment/types-of-brain-tumour-adult/glioblastoma/glioblastoma-prognosis/", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"f8ea38bde8a2e671\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nHere are the current statistics we have on glioblastoma prognosis: ... Average survival rate: 25% of patients survive more than one year, and <strong>more than 5%</strong> of patients survive five years or more\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"f8ea38bde8a2e671\">>>", "published": "January 22, 2019", "siteName": "www.thebraintumourcharity.org"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"76f9eef951e9040b\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nGlioblastoma and Increased Survival with Longer Chemotherapy Duration - PMC\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"76f9eef951e9040b\">>>", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6710024/", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"ea9ebf61ec33f9e9\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nThe most prevalent locations of all brain tumors were the frontal lobe (25.6%) and the temporal lobe (19.6%). The CBTRUS also reported the mortality rate from malignant brain and CNS tumors was <strong>3.48 for females and 5.23 for males per 100,000 person-years. The five-year survival rate for GBM is 4.7%</strong>...\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"ea9ebf61ec33f9e9\">>>", "siteName": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"7254222dbabe39dd\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nWhat is the average glioblastoma survival rate? — Glioblastoma Research Organization\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"7254222dbabe39dd\">>>", "url": "https://www.gbmresearch.org/blog/glioblastoma-survival-rate", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"690a28c2ea7a0cef\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nChildren diagnosed with glioblastoma are predicted to have a better survival rate, and <strong>about 25 percent</strong> of kids who have this tumor live for five years or more. A lack of data raises many questions about the most up-to-date information.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"690a28c2ea7a0cef\">>>", "published": "December 1, 2022", "siteName": "www.gbmresearch.org"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"17c7f9f7baa848da\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nGlioblastoma - Survival rates - Mayo Clinic\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"17c7f9f7baa848da\">>>", "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/glioblastoma/survival-rates/gnc-20596050", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"cc3bce602b6d481b\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nGlioblastoma — Overview covers diagnosis and treatment of this aggressive form of brain cancer that can be slowed but usually can&#x27;t be cured.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"cc3bce602b6d481b\">>>", "published": "1 month ago", "siteName": "www.mayoclinic.org"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"119b02725f301828\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nGlioblastoma (GBM): What It Is, Symptoms & Prognosis\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"119b02725f301828\">>>", "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/17032-glioblastoma", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"bdbc8a3aa058c2b3\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nMost people live an average of 12 to 18 months after diagnosis. The five-year survival rate for glioblastoma is only <strong>about 5%</strong>. That means <strong>about 5%</strong> of people with GBM are still alive five years after their diagnosis.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"bdbc8a3aa058c2b3\">>>", "published": "August 30, 2023", "siteName": "my.clevelandclinic.org"}]}, "isError": false, "timestamp": 1775274813170}}
