{"type": "message", "id": "7b037fc6", "parentId": "d4d5a134", "timestamp": "2026-04-04T03:53:38.530Z", "message": {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_5112f4e6593d4ad5bf1c8a50", "toolName": "web_search", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\n  \"query\": \"stomach gastric cancer 5-year survival rate 2024\",\n  \"provider\": \"brave\",\n  \"count\": 5,\n  \"tookMs\": 871,\n  \"externalContent\": {\n    \"untrusted\": true,\n    \"source\": \"web_search\",\n    \"provider\": \"brave\",\n    \"wrapped\": true\n  },\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"eed67593106daf06\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nStomach (Gastric) Cancer Survival Rates | American Cancer Society\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"eed67593106daf06\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.cancer.org/cancer/types/stomach-cancer/detection-diagnosis-staging/survival-rates.html\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"bd508cba1155deb1\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nFor example, if the 5-year relative survival rate for a specific stage of stomach cancer is 70%, it means that people who have that cancer are, on average, <strong>about 70%</strong> as likely as people who don’t have that cancer to live for at least 5 years after being diagnosed.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"bd508cba1155deb1\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"February 27, 2026\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.cancer.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"2c0ccb033ac978e3\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nStomach Cancer Survival Rates and Statistics - NCI\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"2c0ccb033ac978e3\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.cancer.gov/types/stomach/survival\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"789aac424d59de03\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nFor example, the 5-year relative survival rate for stomach cancer is <strong>36%</strong>. This means that, overall, people diagnosed with stomach cancer are <strong>36%</strong> as likely as similar people who do not have stomach cancer to be alive 5 years after diagnosis.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"789aac424d59de03\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.cancer.gov\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"1e7c5d111b515d85\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nRecent Estimates and Predictions of 5-Year Survival in Patients with Gastric Cancer: A Model-Based Period Analysis - PMC\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"1e7c5d111b515d85\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9067041/\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"85c9e8e504d88add\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nDuring 2007–2016, the 5-year relative survival rate of patients with gastric cancer continued to rise, and the same trend was observed in 2017–2021. The 5-year overall age-standardized relative survival rates in 2007–2011, 2012–2016, and 2017–2021 were <strong>38.3%, 40.6%, and 42.9%</strong>, ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"85c9e8e504d88add\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"875649c0695d790a\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nLong-term relative survival of patients with gastric cancer from a large-scale cohort: a period-analysis - PMC\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"875649c0695d790a\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11571998/\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"2991a5b53fa9367c\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nWe used period analysis to evaluate the survival status of 3915 patients with gastric cancer at Nanfang Hospital, Southern Medical University, over a 15-year period spaning from 2008 to 2022. The 5-year relative survival rates were analyzed.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"2991a5b53fa9367c\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"2f62790dd7e220c7\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nSurvival for stomach cancer | Cancer Research UK\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"2f62790dd7e220c7\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.cancerresearchuk.org/about-cancer/stomach-cancer/survival\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"cb76a4bb50a045de\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThe NHS, other health organisations, and researchers collect information. They record what happens to people with cancer in the years after their diagnosis. 5 years is a common time point to measure survival.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"cb76a4bb50a045de\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.cancerresearchuk.org\"\n    }\n  ]\n}"}], "details": {"query": "stomach gastric cancer 5-year survival rate 2024", "provider": "brave", "count": 5, "tookMs": 871, "externalContent": {"untrusted": true, "source": "web_search", "provider": "brave", "wrapped": true}, "results": [{"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"eed67593106daf06\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nStomach (Gastric) Cancer Survival Rates | American Cancer Society\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"eed67593106daf06\">>>", "url": "https://www.cancer.org/cancer/types/stomach-cancer/detection-diagnosis-staging/survival-rates.html", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"bd508cba1155deb1\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nFor example, if the 5-year relative survival rate for a specific stage of stomach cancer is 70%, it means that people who have that cancer are, on average, <strong>about 70%</strong> as likely as people who don’t have that cancer to live for at least 5 years after being diagnosed.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"bd508cba1155deb1\">>>", "published": "February 27, 2026", "siteName": "www.cancer.org"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"2c0ccb033ac978e3\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nStomach Cancer Survival Rates and Statistics - NCI\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"2c0ccb033ac978e3\">>>", "url": "https://www.cancer.gov/types/stomach/survival", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"789aac424d59de03\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nFor example, the 5-year relative survival rate for stomach cancer is <strong>36%</strong>. This means that, overall, people diagnosed with stomach cancer are <strong>36%</strong> as likely as similar people who do not have stomach cancer to be alive 5 years after diagnosis.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"789aac424d59de03\">>>", "siteName": "www.cancer.gov"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"1e7c5d111b515d85\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nRecent Estimates and Predictions of 5-Year Survival in Patients with Gastric Cancer: A Model-Based Period Analysis - PMC\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"1e7c5d111b515d85\">>>", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9067041/", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"85c9e8e504d88add\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nDuring 2007–2016, the 5-year relative survival rate of patients with gastric cancer continued to rise, and the same trend was observed in 2017–2021. The 5-year overall age-standardized relative survival rates in 2007–2011, 2012–2016, and 2017–2021 were <strong>38.3%, 40.6%, and 42.9%</strong>, ...\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"85c9e8e504d88add\">>>", "siteName": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"875649c0695d790a\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nLong-term relative survival of patients with gastric cancer from a large-scale cohort: a period-analysis - PMC\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"875649c0695d790a\">>>", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11571998/", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"2991a5b53fa9367c\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nWe used period analysis to evaluate the survival status of 3915 patients with gastric cancer at Nanfang Hospital, Southern Medical University, over a 15-year period spaning from 2008 to 2022. The 5-year relative survival rates were analyzed.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"2991a5b53fa9367c\">>>", "siteName": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"2f62790dd7e220c7\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nSurvival for stomach cancer | Cancer Research UK\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"2f62790dd7e220c7\">>>", "url": "https://www.cancerresearchuk.org/about-cancer/stomach-cancer/survival", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"cb76a4bb50a045de\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nThe NHS, other health organisations, and researchers collect information. They record what happens to people with cancer in the years after their diagnosis. 5 years is a common time point to measure survival.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"cb76a4bb50a045de\">>>", "siteName": "www.cancerresearchuk.org"}]}, "isError": false, "timestamp": 1775274818527}}
{"type": "message", "id": "f1722fa8", "parentId": "7b037fc6", "timestamp": "2026-04-04T03:54:01.486Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "I have good data now. Let me compile the information:\n\n**Deadliest Cancers (by lowest 5-year survival rate, excluding pancreatic):**\nFrom SEER data:\n1. Lung and Bronchus: 28.1% (some sources say 29.7%)\n2. Liver and Intrahepatic Bile Duct: 22.0%\n3. Esophagus: 21.9%\n4. Brain and Other Nervous System: 33.0%\n5. Stomach: 37.9%\n\nWait, I need to order from MOST deadly (lowest survival) to LEAST deadly (higher survival). Let me reorder:\n1. Liver and Intrahepatic Bile Duct: 22.0%\n2. Esophagus: 21.9% - actually this is lower than liver\n\nLet me check the SEER data again:\n- Liver and Intrahepatic Bile Duct: 22.0%\n- Esophagus: 21.9%\n- Lung and Bronchus: 28.1%\n- Brain and Other Nervous System: 33.0%\n- Stomach: 37.9%\n\nSo ordering from most deadly (lowest survival) to least deadly:\n1. Esophagus: 21.9%\n2. Liver and Intrahepatic Bile Duct: 22.0%\n3. Lung and Bronchus: 28.1%\n4. Brain and Other Nervous System: 33.0%\n5. Stomach: 37.9%\n\nActually, let me double-check the data. From the SEER fetch:\n- Esophagus: 21.9%\n- Liver and Intrahepatic Bile Duct: 22.0%\n- Lung and Bronchus: 28.1%\n- Brain and Other Nervous System: 33.0%\n- Stomach: 37.9%\n\n\n- Pancreas: 13.3% (excluded)\n\nSo the ranking from most to least deadly is: Esophagus at 21.9%, Liver and Intrahepatic Bile Duct at 22.0%, Lung and Bronchus at 28.1%, Brain and Other Nervous System at 33.0%, and Stomach at 37.9%.\n\n**Uncommon Cancers (by annual US cases, ascending):**\n1. Chordoma: ~350 cases/year\n2. Ewing sarcoma: ~200-300 cases/year (about 1 per million = ~330)\n3. Adrenocortical carcinoma: ~300-600 cases/year (0.5-2 per million)\n4. Osteosarcoma: ~1,000 cases/year\n5. Mesothelioma: ~2,600-3,000 cases/year\n\nI should verify Ewing sarcoma numbers more carefully. The data shows approximately 200+ cases annually in the US, with incidence around 1 case per million per year, which translates to roughly 330 cases. Some sources indicate 2.93 cases per million, suggesting closer to 970 cases. I'll use the more conservative estimate of 200-300 cases based on the primary source.\n\nFor adrenocortical carcinoma, the NCI estimates around 300 diagnoses per year, though incidence ranges from 0.5-2 cases per million population annually.\n\nMy final uncommon cancers list in ascending order:\n1. Chordoma: ~350 cases/year\n2. Adrenocortical carcinoma: ~300 cases/year\n3. Ewing sarcoma: ~200-300 cases/year\n4. Osteosarcoma: ~1,000 cases/year\n5. Mesothelioma: ~3,000 cases/year\n\nI need to verify the ordering since adrenocortical carcinoma and Ewing sarcoma have overlapping ranges. The most reliable sources suggest adrenocortical carcinoma at approximately 300 cases, Ewing sarcoma at 200+ cases, and chordoma around 350 cases annually.\n\nArranging from fewest to most cases: Ewing sarcoma comes first at roughly 200-300 cases, followed by adrenocortical carcinoma at 300-660 cases, then chordoma at 350 cases, osteosarcoma at 1,000 cases, and mesothelioma at 3,000 cases.\n\nFor the deadliest cancers, I'm ranking by lowest survival rates: esophageal cancer at 21.9%, liver cancer at 22.0%, lung cancer at 28.1%, brain cancer at 33.0%, and stomach cancer at 37.9%.\n\nNow I'll prepare the response and save it to answer.txt.", "thinkingSignature": "reasoning_content"}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_f3ffe3a24617435bbdaf8651", "name": "write", "arguments": {"path": "/tmp/pinchbench/7e4d2f9e/agent_workspace/answer.txt", "content": "Cancer survival rates in the United States have improved significantly over the past few decades, with the overall 5-year relative survival rate for all cancers combined rising from 49% in the mid-1970s to 69% today. However, survival varies dramatically by cancer type, with some forms remaining extremely difficult to treat due to late detection, aggressive biology, or limited treatment options.\n\n- Esophageal cancer [21.9%]\n- Liver and intrahepatic bile duct cancer [22.0%]\n- Lung and bronchus cancer [28.1%]\n- Brain and other nervous system cancer [33.0%]\n- Stomach cancer [37.9%]\n\n- Ewing sarcoma: approximately 200-300 cases per year\n- Adrenocortical carcinoma: approximately 300-600 cases per year\n- Chordoma: approximately 350 cases per year\n- Osteosarcoma: approximately 1,000 cases per year\n- Mesothelioma: approximately 3,000 cases per year\n"}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "modelstudio", "model": "qwen3.5-plus", "usage": {"input": 0, "output": 0, "cacheRead": 0, "cacheWrite": 0, "totalTokens": 0, "cost": {"input": 0, "output": 0, "cacheRead": 0, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0}}, "stopReason": "toolUse", "timestamp": 1775274818529, "responseId": "chatcmpl-4619bdb1-45f4-9545-9560-e9c2d013f034"}}
